## The Absolute Architectural Summary
When you look at the entirety of digital infrastructure, security is an interconnected chain. No matter how secure a website's code is, it still relies on:

   1. The Application Code: Which can be broken via logic flaws (IDOR, Parameter Tampering).
   2. The Web Protocol Layer: Which can be exploited via traffic timing (Request Smuggling, Race Conditions).
   3. The Operating System & Hardware: Which can be subverted via memory safety issues (Kernel exploits, Rowhammer).
   4. The Global Internet Routing: Which can be manipulated via protocol trust (BGP hijacking, SS7 redirection).

To protect a system at this absolute scale, organizations move beyond simple coding fixes and employ Defense-in-Depth Frameworks: using encrypted network tunnels (IPsec/VPNs), memory-safe programming languages (like Rust), hardware-level memory protections, and zero-trust routing verification (like RPKI for BGP).
